{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_017KMMQ9nSManf15x5dwozah","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Wrote **`ai-engineering/answers/06-003-diagnose-performance-bugs-in-a-model.md`** — Q3: Diagnose performance bugs in a model.\n\nThe answer follows a **5-step diagnostic ladder**:\n1. **Validate the eval metric** — ensure accuracy/loss aligns with the business goal (imbalanced classes → use PR-AUC)\n2. **Audit the data** — label noise (manual sampling), train/test leakage (timestamps, shuffle seeds), distribution shift (`describe()` comparison)\n3. **Mini-batch overfit test** — force memorization of 32 examples; if training loss won't drop, the training loop is broken (detached tensors, wrong loss, silent broadcasting). Uses `torch.autograd.set_detect_anomaly(True)`.\n4. **Read learning curves** — both high → underfitting; train low/val high → overfitting; val spikes → LR or batch norm bug\n5. **Check train/serve skew** — production feature pipeline differences, `model.eval()` not called\n\nConcrete example: a BERT intent classifier that dropped from 91%→79% because the pipeline started lowercasing text but the tokenizer was `bert-base-cased` — fixed by preprocessing contract alignment, no retraining needed.\n\n23 tasks remaining in the Next queue."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":385,"cache_read_input_tokens":69649,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":385,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2b3ce83-7730-454a-99d7-a975160f9251","uuid":"79283ec9-4389-40a5-8030-b1ddcec207bb"}
